It was split out of the Sepal repository (https://github.com/bloomifycafe/Sepal, Apache-2.0, commit f846e8d) with the git history of
the files that moved. A few small modules of Sepal that the Python front end needs are copied into sepal_runner/shared/ (each names its
origin in its first lines): the record conversion (sepal/train/unsloth_convert.py, socdecide/schema.py), the request handling
(sepal/serve/decision_api.py), the temperature and softmax post-processing (sepal/train/unsloth_arm.py, sepal/eval/predictor.py) and
the reading of the calibrated temperatures (sepal/eval/calibrate.py, sepal/eval/checkpoint.py).

Third-party code
----------------

Clef (clef-flash, Cloudflare), licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0: the JointSchemaHead and the prompt layout
(joint_schema_model.py) that the engine implements, and the encoder that sepal_runner/encode.py reimplements on the tokenizers library.

Portions of this software are adapted from Kev (https://github.com/jaredpalmer/kev, commit 5e42a7a), Copyright its authors, licensed
under the Apache License, Version 2.0. Each adapted file names its origin in its first line. The adapted files are:

  sepal_runner/refengine/attention.py
  sepal_runner/refengine/deltanet.py
  sepal_runner/refengine/executor.py
  sepal_runner/refengine/readout.py
  sepal_runner/shared/temperatures.py     (from sepal/eval/calibrate.py, which is adapted from Kev)

Strata (MIT) and HyperQwen (Apache-2.0) were read as design references (see runner/README.md, Provenance, and runner/cuda/README.md);
llama.cpp (MIT) is used as a benchmark baseline. No code of Unsloth (AGPL-3.0-only) is copied here: Unsloth is only ever run, as a black box, to produce the
oracle that the golden vectors come from (sepal_runner/oracle*.py, tools/runner_*).

The projects the design draws on, with their licences, are listed in Sepal's docs/REFERENCES.md (https://github.com/bloomifycafe/Sepal).
